[Music]  [Music]  february 2022 and ukraine was under  attack  russian forces launched a full-scale  invasion of the country bringing war to  europe for the first time in decades  millions of families were terrified  about what would happen to them they had  to flee their homes to find somewhere  safe to live  and many of them came to neighbouring  countries like poland my parents  are now in ukraine and they stay  there to defend our country we try to  become and  we believe in our country and in our  soldiers a lot in this special edition  of fyi we're hearing from ukrainian  families whose lives have been ripped  apart by war and we're following them on  their long journeys to reach safety  leaving behind their loved ones  i'm terrified of the thought that  someday i will call them and they will  not respond  [Music]  this  is the first time i've seen for myself  what war looks like away from the front  line  [Music]  children  sometimes on their own  traveling across a continent to seek  safety  [Music]  the main crossing points for people  escaping from the war zone is the  village of medica in poland  over there just beyond the border post  is ukraine and you can just see this  flow of people desperate to reach safety  from february onwards millions of people  have made this journey  i've seen them arriving here frightened  and worried about what will happen to  the loved ones they've left behind  very very worried i'm trying to  text them every day every hour because  they're very  scary message from news channels in kiev  it's like  a key of  bombs it's very scary  many of them have been through so much  just to get here witnessing terrible  things during their long journeys  sometimes there are friends from poland  who are here to meet them  and we've seen some emotional reunions  but there are also people in need of  help from complete strangers  to see this mass movement of people it's  really eye-opening i mean i'd seen  pictures and videos on tv of course  but to actually be here and witness it  in person feels completely different i  mean these people have been through the  absolute worst experiences witnessing  war all around them and i can just see  in their faces how exhausted they all  are  susanna from a polish scout group is one  of the people who have been helping out  always with a friendly smile to make the  refugees feel welcome and safe we are so  upset but we don't want to show them to  people because we want them to be braver  we are smiling and we try to give them  our good energy  there are like one kid and she was in  tears when i was giving her sweets and  she gave me chocolate  because she wanted to give me like a  present for  to thank me for this  it's really the little things and people  are just doing whatever they can to help  anyone in any way i mean to carry bags  donating anything and just giving out  little toys and doing anything they can  to cheer up for kids who you know never  had to should never have had to witness  any of the things they've seen  [Music]  in a nearby town a reception center has  been set up in an old supermarket  it's a place where the refugees can rest  and look for advice on finding somewhere  to live  we found a banker from london who was  volunteering as a chef in one of the  food tents  it's a real eye-opener like i'm i'm  absolutely devastated  the inside of that tesco's when we went  in there around midnight 1am there were  kids still awake but most of them are  lined up along the sides like imagine  your grocery counter full of kids just  to sleep in their winter clothes and it  hit around minus 5-6 last night  it really was not a nice  a nice experience  seeing the welcome that refugees have  been given from people all over europe  is great but don't be fooled into  thinking that it's easy being a refugee  through no fault of their own these  families have lost everything  their homes their possessions  their schools  their security  near the railway station we met 17 year  old elia who'd been traveling for 20  hours with her eight-year-old niece  arena and a friend with a one-year-old  they were cold and exhausted so we took  them into a cafe for something to eat  then elia told us why she decided to  flee her home in the ukrainian city of  hakov  we heard the  bomb attack somewhere far away from our  house but then  it was like growing louder and louder my  parents and i just  we just couldn't leave because of our  grandparents and it was impossible but  then the missile  hit our house  [Music]  our ninth floor was destroyed and  that was the moment when i realized that  i gotta go and  i just  couldn't leave my parents alone but i  had no other choice so what was it like  having to leave your parents behind  it's kind of hard for me even to think  of it  because i have my i have tears in my  eyes because i don't know it was like  the most  terrifying experience that i ever had  and when you just need to look in your  parents eyes  when you gotta leave without them  and they don't know how  how your way will be and with who we  will be and where  i don't know but  i guess  i guess it makes it easier that we have  our phones and we just can connect but  i'm terrified of the thought that  someday i will call them and they will  not respond  [Music]  i just can't imagine what that must be  like  always worrying about your mum and dad  and whether or not they're okay  in a city that's been under constant  attack  elia's big dream was to come to the uk  to study international relations at  university  but for now at least that ambition has  been put on hold  because of all of this happening my  dreams are just crushed they are just  destroyed  a polish man we met in the cafe offered  to give the group a lift to the city of  rotsworth more than five hours away  we've all been told about stranger  danger at school  so just like all the people we featured  in this program we checked him out and  saw that he just wanted to help  and not only did he drive them to  vrotswath he also gave them money to  help them find somewhere to live  i kept in touch with elia and you'll see  how she got on later  [Music]  with ukrainian men asked to stay behind  to defend their country nearly all the  refugees are moms and their children  but in the city of krakov we met  twelve-year-old vadim and his 18 year  old sister katarina who were trying to  reach friends in another country  slovakia  their parents like many others had  stayed behind in ukraine they stay there  to defend our country they wanted for us  to come here it's really bad  so i must say that  we have really work in our country  children can't do nothing so i  really back  adults to protest in such way in  anything to stop this world to stop this  really  bad thing in our country  we were so worried about katarina and  vadim so he decided to keep in touch  with them and two weeks on from their  difficult journey to slovakia i've  arranged to hook up with them on my  laptop  hi katarina so how are you after that  long journey from ukraine  i must say that we are almost okay  we are safe  anyway and it's better for us to stay  here now  than in ukraine unfortunately i just  can't choose words to say thank you very  much  because this support is  a very huge and it's great theme that  people from all over the world support  ukrainian in terrible time for us well  we're so glad that you're safe now thank  you so much for talking to us  thank you  [Music]  not everyone who crosses the border from  ukraine has somewhere safe to stay  back at the refugee reception center we  met thirteen-year-old sasha and his mom  karina who'd arrived in poland with no  idea where they would live  i want to woke up and don't hear  guns rockets and  and so on  that's why we come here  luckily they spotted a sign on a car  this couple had driven a thousand miles  from their home in the netherlands to  offer refugees a place to stay we can  bring them to holland and give him a  good stay over there for a long period  once again we wanted to make sure that  sasha and karina would be safe with the  dutch couple and after all the adults  discussed their offer of help it was  decided they would travel to the  netherlands  i'm very happy it's just amazing  they had a long journey ahead of them  and a few weeks later we found out sasha  and karina were really well  after a few weeks living with the dutch  couple they moved into a hostel in the  city of leyden  [Music]  also making a long journey  these refugees were heading to denmark  they will have almost likely never been  to denmark but the danish government is  offering them a safe place to stay  i've heard that it's not been so easy  for refugees who want to come to the uk  but to get to denmark there were no  forms to fill in and the process  happened within just a few hours of them  crossing the border into poland  the journey to reach denmark took 15  hours through the night  [Music]  but at the other end  safety  and the promise of a comfortable home  for as long as they needed it  it's the country's neighboring ukraine  that have taken in most of the refugees  that's moldova romania hungary slovakia  and poland  as for the uk  well our government did also agree to  take in thousands of families but  there's been anger that the process to  let people in has been too slow and too  difficult  it's a criticism i put to the prime  minister boris johnson when braden and i  met him in downing street  we've got an amazing record as a country  for taking people in we're seeing  massive generosity already by the by the  british people but i know that the visa  application process is really really  complicated so how are you going to make  it easier some people have said well you  should get rid of checks all together  and you should just let people in  without any any any chance i just don't  think that is  practical because  at the moment if you look at what's  happening in ukraine it is a nightmare  you've got a lot of people who aren't  necessarily who they say they are  you know some  fast checks i do think are sensible but  i think getting rid of checks all  together is not a good idea  [Music]  there was just one more person i wanted  to catch up with  two weeks after she arrived in the city  of rotswaff i wanted to meet ellia again  to see what had happened to her  so how has life been for you since  moving to rotsworth well i was just  trying to forget all of the nightmare  things that i've been through when you  see the sky with no missiles and with no  smoke  of course you can understand that  it's like a different world and of  course you can't believe it till  the moment when you realize that your  family is still in this other universe  that you've left i know that you were  really worried about your parents so how  are they and how's their home city right  now  now it's almost destroyed and all of the  buildings i used to look at when i was  younger nothing of this is existing  right now it's just destroyed  but  i know that my parents are staying  positive they tell me that everything's  be okay and the missile  won't touch them anyways because it  already did and they say that missiles  don't  hit one place two times  and  it's the only thing that  that i hope will work out and that it is  true and  yeah that they will be saved in the end  of all of this i know you've just had  your 18th birthday it must have been so  strange without your parents around  yeah of course but  you know it was like lightened with the  fact that  i heard their voices yeah i heard their  voices and they were safe and sound and  it was like the best birthday present  that i ever got  so yeah have you been able to make any  decisions about your future  the decision is just not to give up yeah  and just to keep my chin up and to set  up new goals and achieve them well i  think you're so strong and i really do  hope that you get to go to the uk and  study  thank you  [Music]  it was great to meet ellie again and  maybe with her determination not to let  the war crush her dreams  she can play a role in helping ukraine  to have a peaceful future  but in the meantime the nightmare goes  on for the ukrainian people  families remain separated  and people continue to cross the border  just to find somewhere safe to live  i can only imagine what it must be like  for the refugees here having their whole  lives turned upside down overnight  none of these people ever thought that  this would happen to them and we've got  to do what we can to help this is 2022  and we're in europe this shouldn't be  happening it really should not be  happening  [Music]  you

